<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Getúlio Vargas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1882">1882</a>–<a href="/years/1954">1954</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Getúlio Vargas was a Brazilian politician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande-do-sul">Rio Grande do Sul</a> who took power in the Revolution of 1930. He ruled as provisional and then constitutional president, and in 1937 proclaimed the authoritarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a>, centralising government, promoting industry and introducing labour laws. Deposed by the army in 1945, he was elected president in 1950 and shot himself in the presidential palace in 1954 during a political crisis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/getulio-vargas.jpg" alt="Getúlio Vargas" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Governo do Brasil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Getulio_Vargas_(1930).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dictators also ruled Poland after Józef Piłsudski’s coup of 1926, Portugal under António de Oliveira Salazar, Spain under Francisco Franco from 1939, much of the Balkans and the Baltic states, and Brazil under <b>Getúlio Vargas</b> from 1937.</blockquote><a href="/government/dictatorship">Dictatorship · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>On 10 November 1937 <b>Getúlio Vargas</b>, president of the Second Brazilian Republic, closed Congress with army support and imposed a new constitution modelled on European authoritarian regimes, creating the Estado Novo, or New State.</blockquote><a href="/state/estado-novo-brazil">Estado Novo (Brazil)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the 1930 election, <b>Getúlio Vargas</b> of Rio Grande do Sul led the Revolution of 1930, which deposed President Washington Luís and opened the Second Brazilian Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-brazilian-republic">First Brazilian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Getúlio Vargas</b> returned as elected president in 1950 and founded Petrobras, the state oil company, in 1953.</blockquote><a href="/state/fourth-brazilian-republic">Fourth Brazilian Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The Revolution of 1930 ended the First Brazilian Republic and installed <b>Getúlio Vargas</b> as head of a provisional government.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-brazilian-republic">Second Brazilian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
